WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.270
I'm here today to bring you
a mix of optimism and realism.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:16.320
The optimism is that AI will do, and
is already doing, remarkable things.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:22.250
So just yesterday it proved a striking
mathematical theorem that some people called

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.490
"The greatest achievement of AI so far."

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:31.240
It will extend our lifespans, give us
a wealthier society, more entertainment,

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.570
more instruction.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.650
But the realism is no less important.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.900
And the realism is, first of all, a
lot of people won't like what is going to

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:46.480
happen. And second, a lot of the benefits
will come more slowly than advocates

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.720
would like you to think.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.330
So it will feel bad for a while.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.530
I would stress the following point.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:59.650
No one in this room is old enough to
have lived through a radical technological

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.370
revolution.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.740
I believe my grandmother did.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.960
She was born in 1905.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:10.350
She saw the birth of consumer society,
automobiles, airplanes, radio, television.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.490
So much in the first
50 years of her life.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.940
But today we all have grown up.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.090
The Internet was a change.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.490
Computers are more important.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.390
Our lives have not really been disrupted.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:27.220
But what is coming is that virtually
all of us will, in a radical way,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.210
have jobs that are very different
from the jobs we expected.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.860
They might be less routine.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:33.910
They might be more fun.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.470
They might be higher paying.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:36.960
But they will be different.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.020
People do not always like
change and uncertainty.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:45.990
And furthermore, when your kids, your
grandkids, come to you and say, "Grandpa,

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.430
what should I do for a living?

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.140
What skills do I need to learn?"

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.990
You won't actually have
that great an answer.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.720
There was an older world we all grew
up in, where if you were very smart,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.590
went to a good school, worked
hard and followed the rules,

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.050
the chance that you could end up, say,
living in Manhattan with a Partner in a

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.420
consulting firm, law firm, earn
$2 million a year, have a great life,

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:13.780
feel like you're Master of the Universe,
those are actually the people who might

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.980
lose. The people who will win are the
people who are best at taking initiative,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.480
figuring out how AI works, figuring out
how agents work, doing something different.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.990
In academia, Ethan Mollick, you heard
from him before, he took initiative.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.330
The founders of Sana,
they took initiative.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.480
So it will radically
remix status in society.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.930
Again, in a way none of
us have lived through.

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:43.000
And this will hurt because when some
people go up in status, and some go down in

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.670
status, I will tell you, those who lose
suffer more psychologically than those who

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:49.170
gain.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.950
So you may have heard Eric Schmidt
gave this commencement speech,

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.050
I think two days ago.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.240
And when he mentioned AI, the
students started booing him.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.100
AI doesn't poll that well.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.850
It polls least well with young people.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.660
So this will be difficult.

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:07.710
One thing that is not true, AI
will not bring mass unemployment.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.170
But it will change most jobs.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.370
Key feature of AI, there will
just be many, many more projects.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:17.180
More than we can imagine.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.860
They gave these Swedish slides here.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:24.450
This shows more projects.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.650
Sweden is a project.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:26.750
It's a great project.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.120
But there will be two
new, not entirely new,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.330
but two categories of job that will just
go crazy for how many new jobs there will

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.990
be. The first is what I
call running experiments.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.940
So AI will have many, many
new ideas as it does already.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:49.700
Most of all in the area of biomedicine,
we will need to test those ideas.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.200
In the world today, we're like
"Only PhDs run experiments."

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.060
That's on the way out.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.240
We don't have enough PhDs, or
they want to do other things.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:02.830
So many of us will be testing
ideas of AI. Some ideas of AI,

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:04.570
we'll figure it out through simulation.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.440
So a lot of coding, it's
a kind of internal system.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.960
The AI figures out how
good the AI is doing.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.440
But most things we want
to test in the real world.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.060
So huge new job category,
running experiments.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.410
Another huge new job category is just
gathering data so the AIs can do other

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:27.020
things. Mercor puts out an ad.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:32.630
They want people trained in basketball
analytics to teach the AI how to watch a

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.940
basketball game on TV or live,
and do basketball analytics.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.120
That is the act of gathering data.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.960
Most of the world is not currently
expressed in the form of data.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:47.710
Insofar as we turn the world into
data, AI can do much more for us.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.310
That process will not end anytime soon.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.580
Huge new job category.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.470
Energy and compute.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.000
We need to build those out.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:57.470
Is it going to be nuclear fusion?

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:58.570
Will it be something else?

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:03.150
Will it be installing a lot, lot, lot
more solar panels on the roofs of homes or in

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.400
the desert? Whatever you think
it will be, a lot more jobs.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.440
So it's a wealthier society.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:10.990
There is still scarcity.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.250
There will be more entertainment,
more instruction,

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.700
more artists working
with AI. So believe me,

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:20.070
mass unemployment will not be a problem.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:25.190
What might be a problem is you may not
like having to adjust to the world of AI.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.250
I'm in academia.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.470
I speak to my colleagues all the time.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.230
On average, they do not like it.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.780
They have mastered a skill,
publishing research papers,

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.030
and now they're supposed
to learn some other skill,

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:41.050
working with the AI to create something
research-like that embodies knowledge.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.630
We'll see how much they enjoy that.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.070
But I think in general, tech and healthcare
will be a huge growth sector.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.200
Healthcare forever.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.220
People can never live
long enough to be happy.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:57.630
Tech. Anthropic is still hiring.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.550
I asked Sam Altman in
my podcast with him, "Sam,

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:05.220
when will the finance department
of OpenAI be 80% agents?"

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.350
He said, "Not anytime soon."

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:12.020
So tech will continue to grow, though
many, many jobs will be taken away,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:13.630
as already been presented here.

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:20.170
But just—healthcare. My quite subjective
opinion, but I think over the next 30 to

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:24.670
40 years, most of the things
that kill you, we will fix.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.060
You might die in accidents.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.270
You'll probably still die of old age.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.220
I'm not sure what we can do
with your brain past some point,

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.580
but I'm here to tell you,
buckle your seatbelt.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.000
Don't go bungee jumping, whatever.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:42.400
This is tough for me.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.340
What's really the time horizon here?

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.990
I'm 64, so I'm exercising more.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:52.260
When I hear, "Oh, it all might be great
within 30 to 40 years," I'm like, "I'm 64!"

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:55.980
My daughter, that's wonderful for her.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.300
The grandkids, they're ages 3 and 4.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.590
Incredible for them.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:01.610
Can I hang on to 84?

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:02.630
Well, I think so.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.480
Can I hang on to 94?

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.770
Well, maybe along the way,
AI will help me again.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:11.590
I'll get to 94, then I'll get to
99, and then finally, that will be it.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.970
But that will be, in essence, the way
AI will be sold to the public because it

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.050
won't be that popular.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.410
It will help us against China
and in foreign conflict,

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.930
and we will live much longer and fix many
things that go wrong with our bodies and

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:33.320
maybe our minds, and along the way, feel
disoriented and sometimes demoralized,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.980
but we will live through one of the most
fantastic chapters of human history ever.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:40.200
So be ready.

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:42.700
I would just say don't
be too discouraged.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.840
The benefits are truly large.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:46.560
But here's one of my views.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.800
People I know in Silicon Valley,
they make fun of me for this view.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:51.510
They think I'm crazy.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:52.900
And you know what?

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.260
I think they're crazy.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:56.660
So here's the view.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:08:01.050
My view is that AI in the medium
term, certainly in the short term,

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:07.360
it will boost the economic growth rate
from an average of about 2% to about

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:08.680
2.5%.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.400
People are like, "Well, if AI
is so powerful, if it's so smart,

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.040
if it does these amazing things,
proves these math theorems,

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:17.580
how is it that it only gets us to

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:23.420
2.5%?" And the problem is what
I call the human bottlenecks.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.010
The smarter the AI is, the harder
it is for you to work with it,

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.720
the harder it is to get
organizations to adopt it,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:36.130
the harder it is to figure out how
do you integrate AI into an institution.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.210
Right now, we have all this
freelancing, people on their own.

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:40.600
They may not even speak up.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:42.180
I had to write this memo.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:43.530
It used to take me five hours.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:46.700
Now it took me five minutes
working with Claude, right?

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.920
Well, that's great. It gets
converted into leisure time.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.270
I'm all for leisure time.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:55.720
But it's not really converted into higher
productivity for the organization because

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:02.230
the organizations still are not set
up to take those gains and have them with

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:06.760
people making each other more productive
because a whole set of jobs is changed and

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.450
those people do other things and a bunch
of things happen much more quickly and

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.660
then we get to the next thing much more
quickly and the whole company is more

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:14.290
productive.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:16.860
That's already happening in coding.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.450
It's happening in a lot of startups
in so much of the American economy.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:21.630
That is not happening.

00:09:22.748 --> 00:09:25.338
So when I say we go from two
to two and a half percent,

00:09:25.378 --> 00:09:30.388
I'm not bearish on the AI. I wouldn't
say I'm bearish on the humans,

00:09:30.968 --> 00:09:32.698
but we're holding it up a bit.

00:09:33.258 --> 00:09:38.077
I actually think that's reasonably stabilizing
to go from two to two and a half

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:41.108
percent. And some of my Silicon
Valley friends are like, "What,

00:09:41.108 --> 00:09:44.938
you don't think we're going to grow 20
percent, 40 percent, 200 percent in a year?"

00:09:45.287 --> 00:09:48.818
And I'm like, "No, I don't
think we're going to do that.

00:09:48.818 --> 00:09:50.378
I don't think we can do that."

00:09:51.178 --> 00:09:54.647
On average, it takes the FDA
10 years to approve a new drug.

00:09:55.258 --> 00:09:58.988
How frequently in this country are
new nuclear power reactors approved?

00:09:59.418 --> 00:10:02.618
Until recently, there hadn't been,
I believe, one approved for decades.

00:10:03.698 --> 00:10:05.938
So that is the stickiness of the world.

00:10:06.018 --> 00:10:09.478
It is in many ways unfortunate,
in some ways stabilizing,

00:10:09.837 --> 00:10:14.038
and we are combining this radical
new technology with that stickiness.

00:10:14.397 --> 00:10:18.188
The nice thing about getting from
two percent to two and a half percent,

00:10:18.688 --> 00:10:21.918
if you do budget calculations, you
all know about the US debt, right?

00:10:21.918 --> 00:10:23.588
Like you feel, we're screwed.

00:10:23.828 --> 00:10:25.588
My kids are screwed.
Grandkids are screwed.

00:10:25.588 --> 00:10:28.398
Right now, I think debt,
it's over 38 trillion.

00:10:29.017 --> 00:10:30.978
It might have hit 39 trillion.

00:10:30.978 --> 00:10:33.228
What does that number even mean, right?

00:10:33.228 --> 00:10:34.928
Can we conceive it?

00:10:34.928 --> 00:10:38.848
But if our economy can grow two and
a half percent instead of two percent,

00:10:39.398 --> 00:10:43.018
that debt, rather than exploding
and making us the next Greece,

00:10:43.458 --> 00:10:46.388
that debt actually converges
to a manageable level.

00:10:47.247 --> 00:10:53.168
So the way to get out of this hole, like
if you work in AI, you are our savior.

00:10:53.938 --> 00:10:57.398
You're our plan A, that we're
going to grow a bit faster,

00:10:58.238 --> 00:11:00.458
and we're not going to
see a huge tax increase.

00:11:00.458 --> 00:11:03.428
We're not going to see our Medicare,
Medicaid benefits taken away,

00:11:03.637 --> 00:11:05.358
our social security ravaged.

00:11:05.938 --> 00:11:10.378
We will see America's fiscal position
converge to something sustainable.

00:11:11.108 --> 00:11:12.708
You know, it's the other piece of news.

00:11:13.038 --> 00:11:20.378
You are our plan A. There is no plan
B. So if you're sitting in this audience as

00:11:20.378 --> 00:11:21.898
our plan A, I applaud you.

00:11:22.808 --> 00:11:24.777
So this is truly important.

00:11:24.777 --> 00:11:28.958
There'll be a lot about AI people don't
like, but what they don't see is that if we

00:11:28.958 --> 00:11:35.038
don't get AI, there is no other obvious
productivity savior on the horizon.

00:11:36.577 --> 00:11:37.828
And we're all kind of screwed.

00:11:37.848 --> 00:11:40.778
So I'm pretty sure it's going
to boost the growth rate.

00:11:41.308 --> 00:11:44.398
Now, there's still some people
like, "Oh, it's all hallucinations,

00:11:44.398 --> 00:11:45.768
or it only does a few things."

00:11:46.348 --> 00:11:48.418
Fewer and fewer people believe that.

00:11:48.668 --> 00:11:53.518
But here's a way to make the half a percent
boost in the rate of growth plausible.

00:11:54.078 --> 00:11:59.938
In the years in this country, 1995
to 1998, we got some very basic software,

00:11:59.958 --> 00:12:03.727
and we got software to do inventory
management for companies like Walmart.

00:12:04.147 --> 00:12:08.298
In those years, our growth rate, it
basically went up half a percentage point.

00:12:08.748 --> 00:12:13.428
So just ask yourself, do you think AI
can do for us what we did in the mid-90s?

00:12:14.308 --> 00:12:18.398
I'm pretty sure the answer is yes,
even if we're stingy with approving data

00:12:18.398 --> 00:12:20.747
centers, even if we make
some other mistakes.

00:12:20.897 --> 00:12:22.918
The mid-90s were very nice.

00:12:22.918 --> 00:12:24.198
There were some nice advances.

00:12:24.388 --> 00:12:25.548
Real wages went up.

00:12:26.577 --> 00:12:28.007
But it wasn't unprecedented.

00:12:28.007 --> 00:12:28.998
It wasn't utopian.

00:12:29.098 --> 00:12:31.758
So all we've got to do
is a repeat performance,

00:12:31.958 --> 00:12:35.488
but make it last for more than three
years, because the smarts behind AI,

00:12:35.818 --> 00:12:37.807
they're not going to
go away in three years.

00:12:38.128 --> 00:12:43.608
So I'm not convinced, but pretty
optimistic on the positive side,

00:12:43.608 --> 00:12:45.298
and I think we're going to get there.

00:12:48.577 --> 00:12:50.638
Household budget.

00:12:50.638 --> 00:12:53.287
This is another thing
that will worry people.

00:12:53.878 --> 00:12:57.948
So economic growth will be higher,
but the gains will go to people who take

00:12:57.948 --> 00:13:02.728
initiative. And you might wonder, what
percentage of the population is that?

00:13:03.538 --> 00:13:08.868
I think people who are quite poor, and
especially in poorer countries around the

00:13:08.868 --> 00:13:10.558
world, they are the biggest gainers.

00:13:11.088 --> 00:13:16.117
They already have access to free diagnostic,
legal, business, consulting advice.

00:13:16.608 --> 00:13:18.228
It hasn't really spread yet.

00:13:18.748 --> 00:13:22.598
In the last six months, I was twice
in rural Africa, in Ghana, South Africa.

00:13:22.598 --> 00:13:25.608
I went around. I asked people,
"Do you have ChatGPT?" They said,

00:13:25.608 --> 00:13:28.018
"What's ChatGPT?" I showed them.

00:13:28.018 --> 00:13:29.387
I downloaded it for them.

00:13:29.648 --> 00:13:32.587
But people in those positions,
not getting good educations,

00:13:32.618 --> 00:13:35.828
not having access to whatever,
they are the biggest gainers.

00:13:36.468 --> 00:13:39.127
The other biggest gainers,
a lot will be immigrants.

00:13:39.127 --> 00:13:41.318
They're people who are
willing to take initiative,

00:13:41.438 --> 00:13:46.328
did not necessarily go to the best schools,
but think creatively about how to use

00:13:46.328 --> 00:13:52.017
AI. The biggest losers are those Masters
of the Universe, who become partners,

00:13:52.017 --> 00:13:55.948
live in Manhattan, or maybe on the other
coast, earn two million dollars a year,

00:13:56.408 --> 00:13:58.378
and played the game by the rules.

00:13:59.438 --> 00:14:02.417
I think in this future, they
will be sent to Houston.

00:14:02.518 --> 00:14:07.228
They'll be an energy company
executive, earning 350k a year.

00:14:07.838 --> 00:14:09.627
Not a humanitarian tragedy.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.780
It may feel that way to those
of you who are New Yorkers,

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:21.030
but I tell you Houston has better taquerias
and actually somewhat worse weather,

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:23.760
but that group—they're
the biggest losers.

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:27.100
I worry greatly about the
politics of that change.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:33.350
On the other hand, you will probably
live to 98 or something like that,

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:36.730
but when you're 37 and being
sent to Houston, again

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:40.330
the living to 98 thing
sounds like a fantasy.

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:41.570
It will be worth it.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:45.160
I desperately hope our society
makes this choice, but again,

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.390
not everyone will feel good about it.

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:49.460
Speed of change.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:50.890
They made this Swedish video

00:14:50.890 --> 00:14:56.420
for me. It's blurry on purpose—like how
much of our society is really efficient.

00:14:56.590 --> 00:15:01.420
I mentioned before I work in higher
education—other than Ethan Mollick and a few

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:01.930
other people,

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:04.040
how well are we adapting to the AI world?

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.120
We sit around, we bitch about cheating.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:07.320
"Oh, the students are cheating.

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.830
We got to stop them from cheating."
UC Berkeley Law School,

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:11.940
they just issued this code.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:15.470
You can't even use the
AI to ask about a new idea.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:16.830
It's crazy.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.280
If AI can help you cheat on
a task, take that seriously.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:25.720
But it means you're teaching people a
task that humans may not be doing in two or

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:30.400
three years. So the cheating is a signal
that the whole system is screwed up and

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:31.670
don't blame the cheaters.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:37.410
Blame the system for teaching the wrong
things to get higher ed, tenured professors

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:38.060
to do.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.040
The adjustment will take a long time.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:44.960
You ask what percent of the American
economy is static or stagnant like that?

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:48.870
Well, there's government, there's higher
education, there's some, but not all,

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:52.300
parts of our health care system,
a lot of the nonprofit world.

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:54.050
You can go on. Make your own list.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:55.290
Add up all those sectors.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:16:02.740
I think it's at least 40% of GDP. So
40%, 50% of our GDP will be very slow to

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:07.420
adjust and that's another reason why I
don't think we're ever going to grow 20% in

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:07.950
a year.

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:12.490
But with some lock-in skill we'll grow
another half a percentage point and avoid

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.650
some kind of fiscal catastrophe.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:17.610
You might wonder how
does all this affect me?

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:18.610
I'm talking to you all.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:21.590
I'm not a GPT, my name's
not Claude, right?

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:26.460
I've reallocated, I'd say,
two-thirds of my own time.

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:28.200
I do much more mentoring.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:30.910
The AI can't do it in
the same way that I do.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:36.380
I give many more public talks, I work
more with human beings, I travel more.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:41.180
I do less writing—huge
reallocation of my time.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:44.560
I'm old enough I could just sit
on my behind and run out the clock,

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:46.490
but I don't want to do that.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:50.160
This is like people talking
trying to inspire others.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:51.560
Charisma will matter more.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.250
How you look will matter more.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:57.480
The egghead class, I think, will
take a tumble in status again.

00:16:57.670 --> 00:16:59.840
It will be a very weird, strange world.

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:06.030
Being in the world and being human and
being physical will be more the thing.

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:07.880
It will be more what our lives are about.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.730
If you're at this talk, it may not
be exactly the world you grew up in.

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:15.560
I think for the vast majority of Americans,
it will actually feel more normal,

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:16.400
maybe happier.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.290
Oh, there's too much screen time?

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:22.470
Just have your AI agent manage it for
you and tell you once every two days when

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:25.240
there's something in your WhatsApp
chat that you actually care about.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:26.640
It'll be great.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:31.420
So it will be a return to a
kind of realness and physicality.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.180
Geopolitics: this is
the geopolitics slide.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:39.770
It shows Sweden. I was happy to see
that—an important country you should all

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:43.390
visit. They have amazing
pizza and get this.

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:47.720
They serve cabbage with it
and the cabbage is wonderful.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:49.850
And then there's lingonberries,
great ice cream…

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:51.700
So, go to Stockholm.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:56.900
But countries that do not have AI
are at risk of losing their sovereignty.

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:00.080
There might be good enough open
source models that they can adapt.

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:05.070
But for the European Union in particular,
but much of the world other than America

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:08.780
and China, there's a real chance
they will be dependent on our system,

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:09.840
the Chinese system.

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:14.030
The world will be divided increasingly
into two camps: US and China.

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:15.760
Countries will have to choose.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:19.080
This is not bad for
the US, but very dicey.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.470
I would just say: start
thinking about that world.

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:25.850
Our last slide, world of leisure,
yes, but here's the other final kicker.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:28.000
Right now you have to work harder.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.840
There's new terms of competition,
you have to adjust, you have to learn,

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:35.480
just keeping up with AI. I used to be
so excited when a new model would come out.

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:37.660
Now I'm like, "Oh, my calendar
can't handle it.

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:39.430
Help! please postpone it a week."

00:18:39.950 --> 00:18:43.690
So more leisure in the long term, a
lot more work and some stress in the short

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:49.460
term, and I hope you all follow and watch
this world and accompany me moving into

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:51.290
it. Thank you all for
coming and listening.

